WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.160
Before the barcode, every price at checkout had to be typed by hand.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:10.300
Afterward, a laser-read and identity printed in ordinary ink for a cost approaching zero.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.160
This is First Principles Daily, episode 67.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:20.180
In the early 1970s, a supermarket checkout required a clerk to read each price tag and

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:22.160
press keys on a mechanical register.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.640
One item at a time, the clerk moved through the cart.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.720
The physical limit of that process was set by human reading speed, finger movement,

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.960
and the chance of misreading a label.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.440
A thin pattern of black bars printed on the package changed the floor.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:43.060
The same information became machine readable in a few milliseconds at negligible added cost.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.320
The gap between those two realities is the story of the UPC.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:53.240
Before standardization, every manufacturer printed its own product codes or none at all.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.060
Retailers relied on human clerks to enter prices and descriptions manually.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.900
The convention grew from the assumption that identification would always be performed

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.940
by a person looking at printed text or a price sticker.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.660
That assumption locked the cost structure to the speed of one worker per lane

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:14.080
and to the error rate that came with repeated keystrokes.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:19.740
Inventory counts required shutting sections of the store or paying extra staff to count stock

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:25.600
after hours. Reordering depended on periodic physical audits rather than continuous data.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:30.480
The finished cost of a transaction therefore included minutes of labor per cart.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.520
It also included the carrying cost of overstock or stockouts that resulted from

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.480
slow, inaccurate counts. Retail chains accepted these numbers because every

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:43.280
competitor operated under the same manual constraint.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.360
No single store could change the upstream printing practices of hundreds of suppliers.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.580
The system felt permanent because the tools had not changed in decades.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.800
Cash registers, paper ledgers and human vision stayed the same.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:03.000
Clerks also had to handle exceptions such as unmarked items or price changes by consulting

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.360
paper lists or calling a supervisor. Each step added seconds that multiplied across

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:13.380
thousands of daily transactions. Because the price itself lived only on the package,

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:18.360
any adjustment meant someone had to open every card and resticker individual units.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.580
That process itself introduced new opportunities for mislabeling.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:28.000
The entire chain therefore treated human attention as both the sensor and the database.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:33.780
There was no practical way to separate those functions or to amortize them across more than one

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:40.400
item at a time. The barcode effort began by asking what physics actually requires to move identity

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:46.640
for package to register. The raw material floor is the marginal cost of adding a pattern of dark

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:52.240
and light stripes to an existing printed label. That incremental cost is measured in fractions

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:57.620
of a cent per package once the printing plates are made. The old manual system carried an

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:03.040
effective idiot index set by labor minutes and error correction rather than by any material.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:09.500
The dominant expense was time and attention, not ink or paper. To see the index clearly,

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.700
consider that a clerk might spend several seconds per item reading and typing.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.520
Multiply those seconds by thousands of items per day and the labor cost grows large.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:24.000
The material cost of the price sticker itself stays tiny by comparison.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:30.320
The ratio between the finished transaction cost and the raw material cost therefore sits very high.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:37.480
The 1973 UPC standard collapsed that index by encoding a unique 12 digit number in a fixed

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:43.440
width symbology. A low power laser could read it at any orientation within a few hundred milliseconds.

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:49.000
The first engineering step was to agree on one simple set across every manufacturer.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.720
A single scanner could then serve every product. Without that agreement,

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:58.280
the hardware investment would have remained fragmented. Once the symbol existed,

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:04.360
the scanner itself replaced the human eye and finger. A helium neon laser beam swept across

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:10.500
the bars. Photo diodes measured the reflected light transitions. Simple logic decoded the number

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:16.320
without any keystroke. That substitution removed the per item labor time that had defined the old

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.300
floor. A second move was to print the bars with ordinary flexographic or lithographic presses

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:28.200
already used for packaging artwork. No new materials or separate labeling step was required.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:33.540
The third move was to attach the scanner to an electronic cache register that could look up the

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:39.700
price from a central file rather than from a sticker on each can or box. Each of these changes

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:45.740
attacked a different term in the old cost equation. The standard removed duplication. The optics

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:51.540
removed reading time. The database removed repeated price marking. The tradeoff that had to be

00:04:51.540 --> 00:05:03.380
solved was print tolerance. The bars had to remain scanable even after ink spread and package

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:10.380
capabilities. The 1974 test and marsh supermarket confirmed that a single pack of gum could be read on

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:16.380
the first pass. The physics worked at scale. One obvious objection is that early scanners would

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:22.120
still fail on crushed or wet packages. The design addressed this by adding a check digit that

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:27.640
let the decoder reject a misread and prompt a second pass rather than silently accept an error.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:33.240
Another objection is that small manufacturers might resist the cost of new printing plates.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:37.320
Because the plates were produced on the same equipment already used for artwork,

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:42.700
the added expense stayed small enough that the shared standard could spread without any single firm

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:48.460
bearing a prohibitive share. A further objection is that the database itself would need constant

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:54.680
updates. The system therefore treated price changes as a separate data layer problem rather than a

00:05:54.680 --> 00:06:00.860
per package reprinting problem. Variable cost moved from thousands of physical stickers to one

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:06.880
electronic record. Each step therefore converted a recurring labor or error cost into a one-time

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:12.940
engineering or data cost that could be amortized across millions of reads. Once the symbol and

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:19.300
scanners spread, transaction time per item fell from several seconds of typing to well under a second

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:25.500
of scanning. Continuous sales data replaced periodic stock counts. The new idiot index for

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:31.300
identification itself sits close to the material floor because the incremental printing cost is

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:37.800
now negligible. The scanner hardware is amortized across millions of reads. What remains higher than

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:43.740
the absolute floor is the cost of maintaining the shared item database. Training staff on exceptions

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:49.920
and handling damaged or poorly printed codes also add cost. Labor questions surfaced during the

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:55.990
transition. Some stores reduced the number of cashiers per shift. Others redeployed staff to

00:06:55.990 --> 00:07:02.210
stocking and customer service. The data layer also created new dependencies. When the central file

00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:08.670
was wrong, every lane inherited the error. Accuracy in the master file became a permanent operating cost

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:14.770
that had not existed when prices lived only on the package. Even with these limits, the continuous

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:20.110
stream of item level sales data made possible tighter coordination between stores and suppliers.

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:25.850
Safety stock that had previously buffered against slow, inaccurate manual counts could shrink.

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:32.150
The same data also revealed which products moved fastest. Shelf space could be reallocated

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:37.430
without waiting for end of week ledger summaries. These second order effects were not part of the

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:43.710
original scanner specification. They emerged once the identification step itself no longer required

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:49.290
human transcription at every point. A printed pattern whose only job is to trigger a lookup

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:54.770
can replace minutes of human transcription once the pattern and the reader are standardized across

00:07:54.770 --> 00:08:01.330
an entire supply chain. The same logic that turned variable labor into fixed near zero marginal cost

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:07.130
can be applied wherever identity or state must move from one object to another at high frequency.

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:12.870
Tomorrow the show returns with another concrete case or another domain still operating far

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:19.250
above its physical floor. What everyday transaction still treats human reading speed as an unavoidable

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:24.890
limit. That's first principles daily for today. If this changed how you see what things cost,

00:08:25.390 --> 00:08:30.830
share it with someone who depreciated and subscribe wherever you listen. I'm Patrick and Vancouver,

